that I'm just totally dead guessing.

1215
01:22:21.340 --> 01:22:23.340
Of course, I don't go with fried chicken, because I know that.

1216
01:22:23.340 --> 01:22:25.340
I mean, I'm bad, but I know that's bad.

1217
01:22:25.340 --> 01:22:27.340
You know, fries no good.

1218
01:22:27.340 --> 01:22:32.340
But if it's baked chicken, oh, man, that's the meal I'm taking.

1219
01:22:32.340 --> 01:22:36.340
Now, if chicken's not there, I pick fish.

1220
01:22:36.340 --> 01:22:39.340
But I don't pick shellfish, like mobsters and black,

1221
01:22:39.340 --> 01:22:41.340
because they're high in cholesterol.

1222
01:22:41.340 --> 01:22:44.340
So I pick just the regular fish, the non...

1223
01:22:44.340 --> 01:22:47.340
the scale fish, not the shellfish.

1224
01:22:47.340 --> 01:22:55.340
Number two, never pick casseroles for children.

1225
01:22:55.340 --> 01:22:57.340
If they ask you, what's a good meal for this child,

1226
01:22:57.340 --> 01:23:00.340
and it's a little kid, don't pick a casserole.

1227
01:23:00.340 --> 01:23:04.340
Tuna, tofu, and albalfa.

1228
01:23:04.340 --> 01:23:05.340
Casserole.

1229
01:23:05.340 --> 01:23:08.340
It may be wonderfully nutritious, but they won't eat it.

1230
01:23:08.340 --> 01:23:10.340
So don't pick a casserole for them.

1231
01:23:10.340 --> 01:23:13.340
Number two, never mix medication in children's food.

1232
01:23:13.340 --> 01:23:18.340
By the way, before you mix medication in anyone's food,

1233
01:23:18.340 --> 01:23:20.340
what must you ask for?

1234
01:23:20.340 --> 01:23:22.340
Permission.

1235
01:23:22.340 --> 01:23:25.340
Never Mickey Finn patient's food.

1236
01:23:25.340 --> 01:23:27.340
And I know that when you...

1237
01:23:27.340 --> 01:23:32.340
I worked in nursing's homes, and I've Mickey Finned 90% of my patient's food.

1238
01:23:32.340 --> 01:23:35.340
But on the boards, I won't do it.

1239
01:23:35.340 --> 01:23:39.340
Toddlers, it's finger food.

1240
01:23:39.340 --> 01:23:41.340
It's finger food.

1241
01:23:42.340 --> 01:23:45.340
In other words, what's a better meal for a toddler?

1242
01:23:45.340 --> 01:23:51.340
Hot dogs and french fries, or a tofu bean curd and albalfa sprout salad.

1243
01:23:51.340 --> 01:23:56.340
The hot dog and the french fries, even though the other one is much more nutritious.

1244
01:23:56.340 --> 01:23:59.340
See, when they give you a toddler question, it's not about nutrition content,

1245
01:23:59.340 --> 01:24:01.340
it's about what can they eat on the run.

1246
01:24:01.340 --> 01:24:07.340
So it may be not very healthy, but it's finger food, so go for it.

1247
01:24:07.340 --> 01:24:10.340
I have the last thing.

1248
01:24:10.340 --> 01:24:12.340
They always say...

1249
01:24:12.340 --> 01:24:18.340
I love statistics and research projects, but this big research project came out of Ann Arbor.

1250
01:24:18.340 --> 01:24:21.340
Well, I should have known already.

1251
01:24:21.340 --> 01:24:29.340
That hot dogs and popcorn are the top two things that toddlers choke on.

1252
01:24:29.340 --> 01:24:31.340
Duh!

1253
01:24:31.340 --> 01:24:35.340
One of the top two things that toddlers eat.

1254
01:24:35.340 --> 01:24:38.340
Hot dogs and popcorn.

1255
01:24:38.340 --> 01:24:48.340
See, so I was saying, if tofu squares and bok choy were the top two things toddlers ate,

1256
01:24:48.340 --> 01:24:51.340
what would be the top two things they choke on?

1257
01:24:51.340 --> 01:24:54.340
Tofu squares and bok choy.

1258
01:24:54.340 --> 01:24:57.340
So you've got to watch that stuff, but it's just kind of crazy.

1259
01:24:57.340 --> 01:25:01.340
Anyhow, preschoolers, leave them alone.

1260
01:25:01.340 --> 01:25:03.340
Leave them alone.

1261
01:25:03.340 --> 01:25:04.340
What's that mean?

1262
01:25:04.340 --> 01:25:08.340
One meal a day is okay.

1263
01:25:08.340 --> 01:25:12.340
What do you know about toddlers at preschool eating habits?

1264
01:25:12.340 --> 01:25:14.340
They eat when they're hungry.

1265
01:25:14.340 --> 01:25:17.340
They eat when they're hungry, and they may not eat but one meal a day,

1266
01:25:17.340 --> 01:25:20.340
and they eat the same thing every day for seven weeks.

1267
01:25:20.340 --> 01:25:24.340
Then they change it and eat that for seven weeks.

1268
01:25:24.340 --> 01:25:28.340
So what do you do as healthcare workers and parents?

1269
01:25:28.340 --> 01:25:29.340
Leave them alone.

1270
01:25:29.340 --> 01:25:32.340
There's no such thing as anorexia nervosa of preschool.

1271
01:25:32.340 --> 01:25:34.340
They don't say, ooh, I look bad.

1272
01:25:34.340 --> 01:25:37.340
No, they eat when they are hungry.

1273
01:25:37.340 --> 01:25:42.340
Why do they not need the food that the toddler needed?

1274
01:25:42.340 --> 01:25:43.340
Exactly.

1275
01:25:43.340 --> 01:25:44.340
What's the growth curve of an infant?

1276
01:25:44.340 --> 01:25:45.340
Like that.

1277
01:25:45.340 --> 01:25:47.340
What's the growth curve of a toddler?

1278
01:25:47.340 --> 01:25:48.340
Like that.

1279
01:25:48.340 --> 01:25:50.340
What's the growth curve of a preschooler?

1280
01:25:50.340 --> 01:25:52.340
That.

1281
01:25:52.340 --> 01:25:53.340
Who needs more?

1282
01:25:53.340 --> 01:25:57.340
When a kid went from toddler to preschool, what happened to his nutritional needs?

1283
01:25:57.340 --> 01:25:59.340
They bottomed out.

1284
01:25:59.340 --> 01:26:01.340
What do parents always freak out?

1285
01:26:01.340 --> 01:26:03.340
He isn't eating like he...

1286
01:26:03.340 --> 01:26:04.340
He's a preschooler.

1287
01:26:04.340 --> 01:26:05.340
He's a preschooler.

1288
01:26:05.340 --> 01:26:08.340
He's not growing like he used to.

1289
01:26:08.340 --> 01:26:10.340
And parents don't get that.

1290
01:26:10.340 --> 01:26:13.340
That when you shift from toddler to preschool, they're going to stop eating,

1291
01:26:13.340 --> 01:26:14.340
and that's okay.

1292
01:26:14.340 --> 01:26:16.340
One meal a day is okay.

1293
01:26:16.340 --> 01:26:19.340
We have a rule at our house.

1294
01:26:19.340 --> 01:26:21.340
You take one bite of everything on your plate

1295
01:26:21.340 --> 01:26:25.340
because you don't have to eat anything else if you don't want to.

1296
01:26:25.340 --> 01:26:27.340
But you have to take one bite of everything.

1297
01:26:27.340 --> 01:26:30.340
And if that's when you're done, you're done.

1298
01:26:30.340 --> 01:26:33.340
My brother, who is a pediatrician who should have known better,

1299
01:26:33.340 --> 01:26:37.340
he had kids too.

1300
01:26:37.340 --> 01:26:41.340
And his rule was you eat everything on your plate.

1301
01:26:41.340 --> 01:26:48.340
And you are not getting off this table until everything your plate is licked clean.

1302
01:26:48.340 --> 01:26:57.340
It is really, really interesting that my daughters are all

1303
01:26:57.340 --> 01:27:01.340
perfect weight for size.

1304
01:27:01.340 --> 01:27:02.340
I mean, they're all...

1305
01:27:02.340 --> 01:27:06.340
They could walk through the hourglass figure for the, you know,

1306
01:27:06.340 --> 01:27:09.340
perfect 20-year-old female.

1307
01:27:09.340 --> 01:27:13.340
Not an ounce of fat here or there that shouldn't be there.

1308
01:27:13.340 --> 01:27:15.340
His kids are...

1309
01:27:15.340 --> 01:27:18.340
You know what I'm talking about?

1310
01:27:18.340 --> 01:27:23.340
And I'm just saying, I have my suspicions of where that started

1311
01:27:24.340 --> 01:27:27.340
because in their lives, what made them good and obedient?

1312
01:27:27.340 --> 01:27:28.340
Food.

1313
01:27:28.340 --> 01:27:32.340
And food was what reinforced if you were good or bad,

1314
01:27:32.340 --> 01:27:34.340
obedient or disobedient.

1315
01:27:34.340 --> 01:27:36.340
And so, do you know what I'm saying?

1316
01:27:36.340 --> 01:27:39.340
In my kids, food was just food.

1317
01:27:39.340 --> 01:27:41.340
You know, so I don't know. It's interesting.

1318
01:27:41.340 --> 01:27:42.340
Okay, who cares?

1319
01:27:42.340 --> 01:27:44.340
Let's move on.

1320
01:27:44.340 --> 01:27:46.340
Pharmacology.

1321
01:27:46.340 --> 01:27:49.340
But I'm just telling you that for your own thoughts, okay?

1322
01:27:49.340 --> 01:27:52.340
Pharmacology, I have to guess all the time in pharma.

1323
01:27:52.340 --> 01:27:56.340
The most commonly tested area in pharma is side effects.

1324
01:27:56.340 --> 01:27:58.340
You do not have to memorize dosages.

1325
01:27:58.340 --> 01:28:01.340
Please don't do that.

1326
01:28:01.340 --> 01:28:03.340
Don't memorize routes.

1327
01:28:03.340 --> 01:28:06.340
Don't memorize frequencies.

1328
01:28:06.340 --> 01:28:09.340
If you're going to do any memorization of drugs,

1329
01:28:09.340 --> 01:28:11.340
please memorize side effects.

1330
01:28:11.340 --> 01:28:14.340
Why?

1331
01:28:14.340 --> 01:28:16.340
Because you are a nurse.

1332
01:28:16.340 --> 01:28:20.340
Your job is to assess for side effects

1333
01:28:20.340 --> 01:28:22.340
and see if things are working.

1334
01:28:22.340 --> 01:28:26.340
Your job is not to prescribe the dose,

1335
01:28:26.340 --> 01:28:29.340
prescribe the frequency, prescribe the route.

1336
01:28:29.340 --> 01:28:32.340
Now, there are some where they might give you a dose

1337
01:28:32.340 --> 01:28:34.340
that makes sense or doesn't make sense.

1338
01:28:34.340 --> 01:28:35.340
You know what I'm saying?

1339
01:28:35.340 --> 01:28:39.340
Like, ditched 10,000 milligrams, you know, that kind of thing.

1340
01:28:39.340 --> 01:28:41.340
So, number two.

1341
01:28:41.340 --> 01:28:44.340
Number two, if you know what a drug does

1342
01:28:44.340 --> 01:28:48.340
but you don't know the side effects, how should you proceed?

1343
01:28:49.340 --> 01:28:50.340
Have you ever been in that situation?

1344
01:28:50.340 --> 01:28:52.340
You go, oh, I know that drug.

1345
01:28:52.340 --> 01:28:53.340
It's protonics.

1346
01:28:53.340 --> 01:28:54.340
It's a proton pump inhibitor.

1347
01:28:54.340 --> 01:28:56.340
It prevents ulceration in the gut.

1348
01:28:56.340 --> 01:28:58.340
And then it says, what are the side effects?

1349
01:28:58.340 --> 01:28:59.340
And you go, oh, I didn't know that.

1350
01:28:59.340 --> 01:29:01.340
If you had asked me what it was, I knew.

1351
01:29:01.340 --> 01:29:02.340
You see what I'm saying?

1352
01:29:02.340 --> 01:29:03.340
Have you ever been there?

1353
01:29:03.340 --> 01:29:05.340
You know the drug but you know the side effects?

1354
01:29:05.340 --> 01:29:07.340
Great guessing rule that would give you the answer

1355
01:29:07.340 --> 01:29:10.340
almost 80% of the time, four out of five times.

1356
01:29:10.340 --> 01:29:16.340
Pick a side effect in the same body system

1357
01:29:16.340 --> 01:29:19.340
where the drug is working.

1358
01:29:19.340 --> 01:29:21.340
Let me illustrate this.

1359
01:29:21.340 --> 01:29:24.340
If you got a question about a drug you,

1360
01:29:24.340 --> 01:29:28.340
let's say you knew the drug was a GI drug

1361
01:29:28.340 --> 01:29:30.340
but you didn't know the side effects,

1362
01:29:30.340 --> 01:29:35.340
would you pick drowsiness, tachycardia, or diarrhea?

1363
01:29:35.340 --> 01:29:37.340
If you knew it was a heart drug

1364
01:29:37.340 --> 01:29:40.340
but you didn't know what the side effects were,

1365
01:29:40.340 --> 01:29:43.340
would you pick drowsiness, tachycardia, or diarrhea?

1366
01:29:43.340 --> 01:29:44.340
Tachycardia.

1367
01:29:44.340 --> 01:29:46.340
If you knew it was a central nervous system drug

1368
01:29:46.340 --> 01:29:48.340
but you didn't know what the side effects were,

1369
01:29:48.340 --> 01:29:51.340
would you pick drowsiness, tachycardia, or diarrhea?

1370
01:29:51.340 --> 01:29:53.340
Drowsiness.

1371
01:29:53.340 --> 01:29:56.340
That works so much for me.

1372
01:29:56.340 --> 01:29:57.340
It's really, really big.

1373
01:29:57.340 --> 01:29:59.340
Doesn't it make sense though?

1374
01:29:59.340 --> 01:30:02.340
If a drug is having a therapeutic effect in certain areas,

1375
01:30:02.340 --> 01:30:05.340
it's also going to have some side effects there too.

1376
01:30:05.340 --> 01:30:09.340
So why would a CNS drug have a problem with your gut?

1377
01:30:09.340 --> 01:30:10.340
You know what I mean?

1378
01:30:10.340 --> 01:30:12.340
That's kind of the question we'd ask.

1379
01:30:13.340 --> 01:30:14.340
What about number three?

1380
01:30:14.340 --> 01:30:16.340
Do you think this might happen to you?

1381
01:30:16.340 --> 01:30:19.340
If you have absolutely no clue what the drug even is,

1382
01:30:19.340 --> 01:30:20.340
you swear you've never heard of it.

1383
01:30:20.340 --> 01:30:22.340
Could that happen?

1384
01:30:22.340 --> 01:30:23.340
Okay.

1385
01:30:23.340 --> 01:30:24.340
You're not dead yet.

1386
01:30:24.340 --> 01:30:26.340
You're close but you're not dead yet.

1387
01:30:26.340 --> 01:30:27.340
Look to see if it's PO.

1388
01:30:27.340 --> 01:30:30.340
If they said it was PO, I think you might have dodged a bullet.

1389
01:30:30.340 --> 01:30:32.340
Two out of three times.

1390
01:30:32.340 --> 01:30:34.340
At least 50-50 you're going to dodge the bullet.

1391
01:30:34.340 --> 01:30:37.340
Pick a GI side effect.

1392
01:30:37.340 --> 01:30:41.340
If it's PO, pick a GI side effect.

1393
01:30:41.340 --> 01:30:43.340
That works about 50-50.

1394
01:30:43.340 --> 01:30:44.340
You say, well, that's not very good.

1395
01:30:44.340 --> 01:30:46.340
It's better than one in four.

1396
01:30:46.340 --> 01:30:48.340
Isn't it?

1397
01:30:48.340 --> 01:30:51.340
So that helps me with some questions,

1398
01:30:51.340 --> 01:30:53.340
particularly the really hard ones.

1399
01:30:53.340 --> 01:30:58.340
Number four, never tell a child that medicine is candy.

1400
01:30:58.340 --> 01:31:00.340
Because if you tell them, well, this is candy, this is candy.

1401
01:31:00.340 --> 01:31:03.340
When they go home, what are they going to think medicine is?

1402
01:31:03.340 --> 01:31:04.340
Candy.

1403
01:31:04.340 --> 01:31:07.340
So when you run out of Halloween candy at Halloween time,

1404
01:31:07.340 --> 01:31:10.340
they're going to pass out Grandma's value.

1405
01:31:10.340 --> 01:31:11.340
Right?

1406
01:31:11.340 --> 01:31:13.340
To all these little kids.

1407
01:31:13.340 --> 01:31:14.340
Another actually happened to my brother.

1408
01:31:14.340 --> 01:31:15.340
He was a pediatrician.

1409
01:31:15.340 --> 01:31:16.340
He was working in Chicago.

1410
01:31:16.340 --> 01:31:20.340
He worked at the Hyde Park Clinic in Chicago.

1411
01:31:20.340 --> 01:31:24.340
In Halloween he was working in the ER area.

1412
01:31:24.340 --> 01:31:28.340
This one little kid came in with these symptoms that looked an awful lot like

1413
01:31:28.340 --> 01:31:30.340
tophronil poisoning.

1414
01:31:30.340 --> 01:31:32.340
You know, antidepressant poisoning.

1415
01:31:32.340 --> 01:31:34.340
And they thought, hmm, that's interesting.

1416
01:31:34.340 --> 01:31:37.340
So then about ten minutes later, another kid came in with the exact symptoms.

1417
01:31:37.340 --> 01:31:39.340
And then an hour later, another kid came in.

1418
01:31:39.340 --> 01:31:42.340
And then about a half hour later, a fourth kid came in.

1419
01:31:42.340 --> 01:31:48.340
What happened was, all four of those kids had gone to the same house.

1420
01:31:48.340 --> 01:31:54.340
And there was a little kid literally passing out his parents' tophronil

1421
01:31:54.340 --> 01:31:57.340
to all the little kids for Halloween candy.

1422
01:31:57.340 --> 01:32:02.340
Because he had been in the hospital the week before and was told it was candy.

1423
01:32:02.340 --> 01:32:04.340
So I thought that was a real interesting...

1424
01:32:04.340 --> 01:32:08.340
He got an article in the American Journal of Medicine on that one.

1425
01:32:08.340 --> 01:32:10.340
Okay.

1426
01:32:10.340 --> 01:32:13.340
OB. What's the ace and spades answer in OB?

1427
01:32:13.340 --> 01:32:15.340
Check the beetle heart rate.

1428
01:32:15.340 --> 01:32:17.340
Yeah, check the beetle heart rate.

1429
01:32:17.340 --> 01:32:19.340
MedSurg.

1430
01:32:19.340 --> 01:32:23.340
What's the first thing you assess in a MedSurg situation?

1431
01:32:23.340 --> 01:32:27.340
What's the first thing you assess in MedSurg?

1432
01:32:27.340 --> 01:32:28.340
Level of consciousness.

1433
01:32:28.340 --> 01:32:29.340
Bingo.

1434
01:32:29.340 --> 01:32:31.340
Not airway.

1435
01:32:31.340 --> 01:32:35.340
Airway is not the first thing you assess.

1436
01:32:35.340 --> 01:32:36.340
Think about a code.

1437
01:32:36.340 --> 01:32:39.340
You're in a code, you're doing basic life support, all righty?

1438
01:32:39.340 --> 01:32:42.340
Or advanced cardiac life support.

1439
01:32:42.340 --> 01:32:44.340
What's the first thing you do?

1440
01:32:44.340 --> 01:32:46.340
Annie, Annie, Annie.

1441
01:32:46.340 --> 01:32:47.340
Or what's your name?

1442
01:32:47.340 --> 01:32:48.340
Are you okay?

1443
01:32:48.340 --> 01:32:50.340
What's that?

1444
01:32:50.340 --> 01:32:51.340
Level of consciousness.

1445
01:32:51.340 --> 01:32:55.340
Then you do your ABCs.

1446
01:32:55.340 --> 01:32:57.340
So ABC doesn't even win there.

1447
01:32:57.340 --> 01:32:58.340
Okay.

1448
01:32:58.340 --> 01:33:02.340
But number two, what is the first thing you do now?

1449
01:33:02.340 --> 01:33:08.340
What's the first thing you do in a MedSurg situation if you're guessing?

1450
01:33:08.340 --> 01:33:10.340
Establish and airway.

1451
01:33:10.340 --> 01:33:16.340
So that will work there.

1452
01:33:16.340 --> 01:33:20.340
Okay, let's turn the page.

1453
01:33:20.340 --> 01:33:23.340
Pediatric guessing skills for growth and development.

1454
01:33:23.340 --> 01:33:26.340
How many of you in here aren't really good at growth and development?

1455
01:33:26.340 --> 01:33:28.340
You're joining me.

1456
01:33:29.340 --> 01:33:33.340
I wish I had these rules that I'm going to give you right now when I was in Peds

1457
01:33:33.340 --> 01:33:35.340
because I'd have done a lot better.

1458
01:33:35.340 --> 01:33:39.340
Because my experience in Peds was, particularly growth and development,

1459
01:33:39.340 --> 01:33:42.340
I could always narrow it down to two.

1460
01:33:42.340 --> 01:33:44.340
And after that I was dead.

1461
01:33:44.340 --> 01:33:48.340
Because it seemed like two ages were reasonable, two tasks were reasonable.

1462
01:33:48.340 --> 01:33:50.340
Here's the deal.

1463
01:33:50.340 --> 01:33:52.340
Three rules of growth and development questions.

1464
01:33:52.340 --> 01:33:58.340
They are all based on the principle, always give the child more time.

1465
01:33:58.340 --> 01:34:01.340
Always give the child more time.

1466
01:34:01.340 --> 01:34:04.340
More time to what?

1467
01:34:06.340 --> 01:34:07.340
Grow and develop.

1468
01:34:07.340 --> 01:34:11.340
In other words, don't rush children's growth and development.

1469
01:34:11.340 --> 01:34:12.340
Don't rush it.

1470
01:34:12.340 --> 01:34:16.340
Always give the child more time to grow and develop.

1471
01:34:16.340 --> 01:34:18.340
So let's talk about these rules.

1472
01:34:18.340 --> 01:34:22.340
Rule number one, when in doubt, call it normal.

1473
01:34:22.340 --> 01:34:24.340
Now this is radically different than Med-Search.

1474
01:34:24.340 --> 01:34:26.340
In Med-Search, what's the rule?

1475
01:34:26.340 --> 01:34:31.340
When in doubt, call it abnormal so you don't make a safety mistake.

1476
01:34:31.340 --> 01:34:35.340
But in growth and development, when in doubt, call it normal.

1477
01:34:35.340 --> 01:34:38.340
So what would be the right answer to the question?

1478
01:34:43.340 --> 01:34:46.340
See, why is the question written the way it is?

1479
01:34:46.340 --> 01:34:48.340
What age did they give you?

1480
01:34:48.340 --> 01:34:50.340
What task did they say?

1481
01:34:50.340 --> 01:34:53.340
What's the problem with that question?

1482
01:34:56.340 --> 01:34:58.340
It varies. It's on the line.

1483
01:34:58.340 --> 01:35:03.340
So some six-year-olds can't read and some six-year-olds can't read.

1484
01:35:03.340 --> 01:35:08.340
So it was purposefully written to confuse you so that you didn't know what to pick.

1485
01:35:08.340 --> 01:35:10.340
And since you didn't know what to pick,

1486
01:35:10.340 --> 01:35:16.340
they were waiting to see if you would give the child more time by calling it normal.

1487
01:35:16.340 --> 01:35:18.340
And that's exactly what they do.

1488
01:35:18.340 --> 01:35:22.340
They purposefully give you ages and tasks where it could go either way

1489
01:35:22.340 --> 01:35:24.340
and ask you what's going on here.

1490
01:35:24.340 --> 01:35:26.340
And so you always call it normal.

1491
01:35:26.340 --> 01:35:28.340
Now what about this?

1492
01:35:28.340 --> 01:35:31.340
You have a 13-year-old who isn't potty trained.

1493
01:35:33.340 --> 01:35:35.340
Did I say call that normal?

1494
01:35:35.340 --> 01:35:37.340
But you shouldn't be in doubt.

1495
01:35:37.340 --> 01:35:39.340
I said when in doubt, call it normal.

1496
01:35:39.340 --> 01:35:41.340
I didn't say to call everything normal.

1497
01:35:41.340 --> 01:35:45.340
That's using your knowledge first.

1498
01:35:45.340 --> 01:35:46.340
Do you understand that?

1499
01:35:46.340 --> 01:35:47.340
Okay, second rule.

1500
01:35:47.340 --> 01:35:48.340
Rule number two.

1501
01:35:48.340 --> 01:35:51.340
When in doubt, pick the older age.

1502
01:35:53.340 --> 01:35:58.340
I don't know about you guys, but whenever they ask at what age should a kid be able to do a certain thing,

1503
01:35:58.340 --> 01:36:00.340
I could always narrow it down to how many?

1504
01:36:00.340 --> 01:36:01.340
Two.

1505
01:36:01.340 --> 01:36:03.340
And I never knew which one to pick.

1506
01:36:03.340 --> 01:36:06.340
When I started using this rule, my scores went through the roof.

1507
01:36:06.340 --> 01:36:07.340
It went way up.

1508
01:36:07.340 --> 01:36:08.340
I picked the older age.

1509
01:36:08.340 --> 01:36:14.340
Not the old desk, but the older age in the two that it could be.

1510
01:36:14.340 --> 01:36:17.340
So in the question here, at what age should a child be able to walk?

1511
01:36:17.340 --> 01:36:19.340
There are two ages where it could be.

1512
01:36:19.340 --> 01:36:21.340
What could those be?

1513
01:36:21.340 --> 01:36:23.340
B and C.

1514
01:36:23.340 --> 01:36:24.340
12 and what?

1515
01:36:24.340 --> 01:36:25.340
14, that's what it was?

1516
01:36:25.340 --> 01:36:27.340
So what would you pick?

1517
01:36:27.340 --> 01:36:28.340
14.

1518
01:36:28.340 --> 01:36:31.340
Because you're giving the kid more time.

1519
01:36:31.340 --> 01:36:33.340
Yeah, some kids could walk at 12.

1520
01:36:33.340 --> 01:36:36.340
But give them a couple more months.

1521
01:36:36.340 --> 01:36:38.340
Are you seeing the principle?

1522
01:36:38.340 --> 01:36:44.340
And then rule number three, when in doubt, pick the easier task.

1523
01:36:44.340 --> 01:36:49.340
I always got questions where they said, a kid is a certain age, what task have they mastered?

1524
01:36:49.340 --> 01:36:51.340
And they'll give me four tasks.

1525
01:36:51.340 --> 01:36:53.340
I could always narrow it down to how many?

1526
01:36:53.340 --> 01:36:54.340
Two.

1527
01:36:54.340 --> 01:36:55.340
And I never knew which one to pick.

1528
01:36:55.340 --> 01:36:59.340
Once I started using this rule, my scores went way up.

1529
01:36:59.340 --> 01:37:02.340
Why do I pick the easier task?

1530
01:37:02.340 --> 01:37:05.340
Because I'm giving him more time to do the harder one.

1531
01:37:05.340 --> 01:37:06.340
Harder one.

1532
01:37:06.340 --> 01:37:08.340
Now, look at a six-month-old kid.

1533
01:37:08.340 --> 01:37:17.340
There are two tasks there that a six-month-old kid probably would be doing, working on, or trying to do.

1534
01:37:17.340 --> 01:37:21.340
What would be those two that they would be working on or trying to do?

1535
01:37:21.340 --> 01:37:22.340
B and C.

1536
01:37:22.340 --> 01:37:25.340
B and C, rolling over and sitting up.

1537
01:37:25.340 --> 01:37:27.340
Okay, so what's the answer?

1538
01:37:27.340 --> 01:37:32.340
Roll over, because that's easier than sit up.

1539
01:37:32.340 --> 01:37:38.340
Do you understand that in growth and development, they always give you two right answers on purpose?

1540
01:37:38.340 --> 01:37:43.340
See, I thought there was one right answer and one wrong one, and I just was stupid and didn't know the difference.

1541
01:37:43.340 --> 01:37:48.340
No, they always give you these, in growth and development in particular, they give you two right answers.

1542
01:37:48.340 --> 01:37:53.340
And they want to see, are you going to call it normal?

1543
01:37:53.340 --> 01:37:59.340
Pick the older age and pick the easier task.

1544
01:37:59.340 --> 01:38:04.340
When you get growth and development questions, what do I want you to be chanting in your head?

1545
01:38:04.340 --> 01:38:07.340
Normal, older, easier.

1546
01:38:07.340 --> 01:38:12.340
Normal, older, easier, okay?

1547
01:38:12.340 --> 01:38:28.340
Literally, my scores on a growth and development test went from an average between 38 and 48 percent to 70 to 78 percent in a day when I started using these rules.

1548
01:38:28.340 --> 01:38:35.340
And I didn't learn any more information, it just, I finally figured out what they were up to.

1549
01:38:35.340 --> 01:38:38.340
Okay, general guessing skills.

1550
01:38:38.340 --> 01:38:40.340
Rule out absolutes.

1551
01:38:40.340 --> 01:38:44.340
Why are absolutes not good answers?

1552
01:38:44.340 --> 01:38:47.340
You have to really apply it to the right answer.

1553
01:38:47.340 --> 01:38:51.340
Yeah, and all you have to do is find one situation where it doesn't apply and it's wrong.

1554
01:38:51.340 --> 01:38:56.340
Now, did I say, never pick an absolute as your answer?

1555
01:38:57.340 --> 01:39:00.340
Because aren't there some absolutes that you should know?

1556
01:39:00.340 --> 01:39:04.340
Like, never push potassium IV.

1557
01:39:04.340 --> 01:39:09.340
Never give a med to a patient unless you can identify, right?

1558
01:39:09.340 --> 01:39:11.340
Those are absolutes and they're correct.

1559
01:39:11.340 --> 01:39:13.340
Why can you pick them?

1560
01:39:13.340 --> 01:39:15.340
Because of your knowledge.

1561
01:39:15.340 --> 01:39:19.340
But I'm saying, if you're guessing, don't pick them.

1562
01:39:19.340 --> 01:39:23.340
Number two, if two answers say the same thing, neither one is right.

1563
01:39:23.340 --> 01:39:28.340
I had a HSE test last spring and it asked me about a disease I'd never heard of before.

1564
01:39:28.340 --> 01:39:30.340
And it asked me, what would you see?

1565
01:39:30.340 --> 01:39:41.340
A said increased bowel sounds, B said something else, C said borberigamy, and D said something else.

1566
01:39:41.340 --> 01:39:45.340
Well, I immediately ruled out increased bowel sounds and borberigamy because they were the same thing.

1567
01:39:45.340 --> 01:39:48.340
Then I figured which one of the remaining ones would be logical.

1568
01:39:48.340 --> 01:39:49.340
I picked it and got it right.

1569
01:39:49.340 --> 01:39:51.340
And that was just a guess.

1570
01:39:53.340 --> 01:39:58.340
Number three, if two answers are opposite, one of them is probably right.

1571
01:39:58.340 --> 01:40:07.340
If two answers are opposite, one of them is probably right.

1572
01:40:07.340 --> 01:40:10.340
Number four, the umbrella strategy.

1573
01:40:10.340 --> 01:40:12.340
I like this one.

1574
01:40:12.340 --> 01:40:14.340
This one helps me out a lot.

1575
01:40:14.340 --> 01:40:18.340
It says, one answer, which answer is more global.

1576
01:40:18.340 --> 01:40:21.340
Have you ever had the experience, and I have it a lot,

1577
01:40:21.340 --> 01:40:26.340
in a test where I want to say all of the above, but that's not there.

1578
01:40:26.340 --> 01:40:27.340
Have you ever had that experience?

1579
01:40:27.340 --> 01:40:30.340
I want to say all of the above, but that's not an option.

1580
01:40:30.340 --> 01:40:36.340
If that's the experience you're having, look for an umbrella answer.

1581
01:40:36.340 --> 01:40:42.340
What I mean by an umbrella answer is an answer that covers, like an umbrella,

1582
01:40:42.340 --> 01:40:48.340
covers all the others without saying it does.

1583
01:40:48.340 --> 01:40:52.340
An answer that's so broad that when you do it, you do all the others.

1584
01:40:52.340 --> 01:40:56.340
So look at our example.

1585
01:40:56.340 --> 01:40:59.340
What is the umbrella answer?

1586
01:40:59.340 --> 01:41:00.340
B.

1587
01:41:00.340 --> 01:41:06.340
B. Because if you do that, you do all the others.

1588
01:41:06.340 --> 01:41:10.340
I was doing a test with a student that had some trouble with the test,

1589
01:41:10.340 --> 01:41:12.340
and we ran across one the other day.

1590
01:41:12.340 --> 01:41:15.340
It said, when you transfer a patient from a bed to a wheelchair,

1591
01:41:15.340 --> 01:41:17.340
what was a meds-pub test question?

1592
01:41:17.340 --> 01:41:20.340
When you transfer a patient from a bed to a wheelchair, what's important to do?

1593
01:41:20.340 --> 01:41:25.340
A. Bring the chair as close to the bed as possible.

1594
01:41:25.340 --> 01:41:30.340
B. Remove the foot pedal from the chair so you don't trip over it.

1595
01:41:30.340 --> 01:41:36.340
C. Use safety and good body mechanics when transferring.

1596
01:41:36.340 --> 01:41:43.340
D. Lead into the bed with the strong foot.

1597
01:41:43.340 --> 01:41:45.340
Well, all of them are correct.

1598
01:41:45.340 --> 01:41:48.340
And I'm going, well, they're all right. They're all right.

1599
01:41:48.340 --> 01:41:49.340
Wait a minute, Mark.

1600
01:41:49.340 --> 01:41:50.340
Look for an umbrella.

1601
01:41:50.340 --> 01:41:55.340
The answer was C. Use safety and good body mechanics when...

1602
01:41:55.340 --> 01:41:56.340
Because all those work.

1603
01:41:56.340 --> 01:41:57.340
Do you see how that works?

1604
01:41:57.340 --> 01:42:01.340
I'm surprised that it works as much as it really does.

1605
01:42:01.340 --> 01:42:02.340
Number five.

1606
01:42:02.340 --> 01:42:07.340
If the question gives you four right answers, right answers,

1607
01:42:07.340 --> 01:42:11.340
and asks you to pick the one with the highest priority.

1608
01:42:11.340 --> 01:42:13.340
Now, you realize that an hour ago,

1609
01:42:13.340 --> 01:42:16.340
we talked about prioritization, right?

1610
01:42:16.340 --> 01:42:18.340
What were we prioritizing?

1611
01:42:18.340 --> 01:42:19.340
Four.

1612
01:42:19.340 --> 01:42:20.340
Four what?

1613
01:42:20.340 --> 01:42:23.340
Different patients.

1614
01:42:23.340 --> 01:42:28.340
When you get a question that's prioritization of four different patients,

1615
01:42:28.340 --> 01:42:31.340
use the rules I taught you an hour ago.

1616
01:42:31.340 --> 01:42:37.340
However, if they give you one patient and ask you,

1617
01:42:37.340 --> 01:42:42.340
which of the following needs is highest priority,

1618
01:42:42.340 --> 01:42:46.340
don't use what I taught you before won't work.

1619
01:42:46.340 --> 01:42:49.340
That's for prioritizing different patients.

1620
01:42:49.340 --> 01:42:51.340
What do you do here?

1621
01:42:51.340 --> 01:42:54.340
Well, you do what I call the worst consequences game.

1622
01:42:54.340 --> 01:42:58.340
Take each option, A, B, C, and D, and ask yourself,

1623
01:42:58.340 --> 01:43:01.340
if I did not do this, what is the worst thing that would happen?

1624
01:43:01.340 --> 01:43:07.340
Then the best answer is the one that has the worst outcome if you don't do it.

1625
01:43:07.340 --> 01:43:08.340
Now, let me...

1626
01:43:08.340 --> 01:43:11.340
I know that sounds crazy, but let me illustrate it in the question below.

1627
01:43:12.340 --> 01:43:16.340
Which of the following is highest priority in caring for a suicidal patient?

1628
01:43:16.340 --> 01:43:18.340
How many patients do you have?

1629
01:43:18.340 --> 01:43:19.340
One.

1630
01:43:19.340 --> 01:43:22.340
So those rules I taught you about before, do those apply?

1631
01:43:22.340 --> 01:43:23.340
No.

1632
01:43:23.340 --> 01:43:25.340
So let's take each answer.

1633
01:43:25.340 --> 01:43:29.340
If you do not give him a tranquilizer, what is the worst thing that would happen?

1634
01:43:29.340 --> 01:43:31.340
It'd be agitated.

1635
01:43:31.340 --> 01:43:32.340
Okay.

1636
01:43:32.340 --> 01:43:37.340
If you don't orient him to the unit, what's the worst thing that would happen?

1637
01:43:37.340 --> 01:43:39.340
He'd get lost and disoriented.

1638
01:43:40.340 --> 01:43:44.340
If you don't put him on suicide precautions, what's the worst that would happen?

1639
01:43:44.340 --> 01:43:45.340
He'd be dead.

1640
01:43:45.340 --> 01:43:49.340
If you don't introduce him to the staff, what's the worst that would happen?

1641
01:43:49.340 --> 01:43:50.340
Wouldn't know anybody.

1642
01:43:50.340 --> 01:43:57.340
So, of being agitated, lost, dead, and not knowing anybody, what's the worst?

1643
01:43:57.340 --> 01:43:58.340
Dead.

1644
01:43:58.340 --> 01:44:01.340
So what's the highest priority?

1645
01:44:01.340 --> 01:44:02.340
Suicide precautions.

1646
01:44:02.340 --> 01:44:04.340
So let's do it with the next one.

1647
01:44:09.340 --> 01:44:13.340
Ask yourself, what's the worst thing that would happen if I didn't have sips of water?

1648
01:44:13.340 --> 01:44:16.340
What's the worst thing that would happen if I didn't have pain meds?

1649
01:44:16.340 --> 01:44:19.340
What's the worst thing that would happen if the side rails were or not?

1650
01:44:19.340 --> 01:44:23.340
What's the worst thing that would happen if I didn't use the Abductor Pill?

1651
01:44:23.340 --> 01:44:29.340
Not just something bad, but the worst thing that would happen.

1652
01:44:37.340 --> 01:44:38.340
What's your answer?

1653
01:44:38.340 --> 01:44:39.340
What's your answer?

1654
01:44:39.340 --> 01:44:40.340
Side rails.

1655
01:44:40.340 --> 01:44:41.340
Side rails.

1656
01:44:41.340 --> 01:44:44.900
Yeah, because if you didn't, what's the worst thing that would happen if you didn't do the

1657
01:44:44.900 --> 01:44:45.900
sips of water?

1658
01:44:45.900 --> 01:44:46.900
The eye.

1659
01:44:46.900 --> 01:44:48.460
Not thirsty, not bad enough.

1660
01:44:48.460 --> 01:44:49.460
They get dehydrated.

1661
01:44:49.460 --> 01:44:50.460
Okay?

1662
01:44:50.460 --> 01:44:54.340
If they didn't have pain meds, they'd be in severe pain.

1663
01:44:54.340 --> 01:44:56.860
If you didn't put the side rails up, what would happen?

1664
01:44:56.860 --> 01:44:57.860
Ball break.

1665
01:44:57.860 --> 01:45:01.620
Hips, head, multiple injuries.

1666
01:45:01.620 --> 01:45:06.420
If you didn't use an abductor pillow, what's the worst thing that would happen?

1667
01:45:06.420 --> 01:45:07.500
Mess up the hip.

1668
01:45:07.500 --> 01:45:14.340
So being dehydrated, severe pain, messing up the hip and everything else, and messing

1669
01:45:14.340 --> 01:45:18.060
up the hip, what's the worst of the worst?

1670
01:45:18.060 --> 01:45:21.900
Messing up the hip and other injuries so the side rails is number one.

1671
01:45:21.900 --> 01:45:24.300
Are you seeing that?

1672
01:45:24.300 --> 01:45:25.780
Okay.

1673
01:45:25.780 --> 01:45:29.380
You could also juxtapose C and D, couldn't you?

1674
01:45:29.380 --> 01:45:35.060
You could say, if I put the side rails up but didn't use the abductor pillow, or I

1675
01:45:35.060 --> 01:45:40.540
used the abductor pillow but let the side rails down.

1676
01:45:40.540 --> 01:45:41.540
You hear that?

1677
01:45:41.540 --> 01:45:42.540
Which would be better?

1678
01:45:42.540 --> 01:45:47.180
Put the side rails up and don't use an abductor pillow or use an abductor pillow and leave

1679
01:45:47.180 --> 01:45:48.300
the side rails down.

1680
01:45:48.300 --> 01:45:51.100
What would be better?

1681
01:45:51.100 --> 01:45:55.740
You get the side rails, you get more hot water for the side rails down.

1682
01:45:55.740 --> 01:45:56.740
Okay.

1683
01:45:56.740 --> 01:46:02.220
On eight, when you're stuck between two answers, read the question.

1684
01:46:02.220 --> 01:46:04.980
Where's the clue to the answers?

1685
01:46:04.980 --> 01:46:05.980
In the question, not the answers.

1686
01:46:05.980 --> 01:46:08.060
It's a quick reading and rereading answers.

1687
01:46:08.060 --> 01:46:09.060
Go back and read the question.

1688
01:46:09.060 --> 01:46:11.060
You probably missed something.

1689
01:46:11.060 --> 01:46:15.020
Okay, the last page.

1690
01:46:15.020 --> 01:46:17.020
Right?

1691
01:46:17.020 --> 01:46:19.780
The Sesame Street Rule.

1692
01:46:19.780 --> 01:46:28.060
You have my permission to use this rule when and only when your only remaining option is

1693
01:46:28.060 --> 01:46:30.580
to do this.

1694
01:46:30.580 --> 01:46:33.580
Okay.

1695
01:46:33.580 --> 01:46:37.900
If you're at that point, what should you use?

1696
01:46:37.900 --> 01:46:38.900
Sesame Street Rule.

1697
01:46:38.900 --> 01:46:42.260
But if you use it before then, you will fail.

1698
01:46:42.260 --> 01:46:48.780
For it's because this is a dangerous one to use but it's the one you use when nothing

1699
01:46:48.780 --> 01:46:49.780
else works.

1700
01:46:49.780 --> 01:46:52.740
Do you remember Sesame Street?

1701
01:46:52.740 --> 01:46:55.340
Three of these things belong with the others.

1702
01:46:55.340 --> 01:46:57.460
One of these things just isn't the same.

1703
01:46:57.460 --> 01:46:58.460
Remember that?

1704
01:46:58.460 --> 01:47:02.940
And they show you three sailboats and a kumquat and you had to figure out which one was different.

1705
01:47:02.940 --> 01:47:03.940
Right?

1706
01:47:03.940 --> 01:47:04.940
Okay.

1707
01:47:04.940 --> 01:47:11.380
Well, right answers tend to be different than the others.

1708
01:47:11.380 --> 01:47:12.380
Why?

